INT. CANNERY WORKERS' HALL - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

A long plank table under a flickering chandelier made of fishing floats. Salted air. Six places set, five plates filled, one empty. Rain batters the windows.

ELSPETH MARROW, 60s, presides at the head. THADDEUS, 50s, her brother, sits at her right, carving a roast with no appetite. YUKI, 30s, a guest in a sharp navy suit, sits across with her hands in her lap. RODERICK, 40s, the cannery manager, loosens his collar. MIRABEL, 20s, the cook's niece, pours wine with a trembling hand. PERCIVAL, 70, deaf, bearded, nods off in his chair.

ELSPETH
We were seven when the whistle blew in 'sixty-three.

THADDEUS
Eight.

ELSPETH
Seven. One of us never came to dinner.

RODERICK
I don't recall.

ELSPETH
No. You wouldn't.

YUKI
Mrs. Marrow, I came to discuss the sale of the cannery.

ELSPETH
You came to eat, dear. The sale is dessert.

MIRABEL
Wine, ma'am?

ELSPETH
Pour, child. Pour as if the glasses were hungry.

Mirabel pours. The wine sloshes. She does not look at the empty place.

THADDEUS
It was a good harvest that year. The fish were thick. We worked three shifts for three weeks, and the sea took only what it was owed.

RODERICK
That's not how I remember it.

THADDEUS
You remember it from the office.

RODERICK
I remember the ledger.

ELSPETH
The ledger is a liar. It says seven and it lies.

YUKI
Mrs. Marrow, whose place is empty?

Percival stirs, mutters, falls asleep again. The chandelier floats sway. Elspeth sets down her fork. She does it slowly, with great ceremony.

ELSPETH
It is the place of the one who is coming.

RODERICK
Nobody is coming.

ELSPETH
The tide is.

Thunder rattles the windows. The lights gutter. When they steady, the empty plate is no longer empty. A single sardine, whole, wet, lies across it, its eye round and gold.

Nobody speaks. Mirabel drops the bottle. Yuki rises. Thaddeus lays down his carving knife and, for the first time, looks at his sister with terror.

THADDEUS
Elspeth. What have you done?

ELSPETH
I invited him.

Out in the dark, beneath the floorboards, something heavy shifts.
